<%# The opening-position hold: a server-rendered transcript would paint
    its oldest rows and jump once the controller runs, so the component
    renders data-pending-scroll and the viewport hides until the
    position lands. %>
<section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-medium" id="opening-position">Opening position</h2>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    A scroll container always opens at the top, and HTML has no way to set a
    scroll position, so a server-rendered transcript would show its oldest
    messages first and jump to the newest once the controller runs. Poetry
    avoids the jump: for <code class="text-foreground">default_scroll_position: :end</code>
    (the default) and <code class="text-foreground">:"last-anchor"</code>, the root and the
    viewport render <code class="text-foreground">data-pending-scroll</code>, the viewport
    stays hidden while the attribute is present, and the controller removes it the
    moment the opening position is applied — or right away when the transcript is empty.
    <code class="text-foreground">:start</code> opens where the browser does and never
    holds.
  </p>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    The hold keeps the frame: the viewport hides with
    <code class="text-foreground">visibility</code>, so the layout exists for the scroll
    and nothing around it moves. Without JavaScript the hold is lifted and the region
    is a plain, readable scroll container. A cached page that Turbo restores gets the
    hold back before it is snapshotted, so returning to a conversation never flashes
    the top of the thread either. Style the state yourself with the
    <code class="text-foreground">data-pending-scroll</code> attribute if you replace the
    viewport's classes.
  </p>
</section>
